var MESSAGE_QUEUE_QUESTION_SECTIONS = {
  "Fundamentals": [
    "## Fundamentals",
    "",
    "### Q1: What is a message queue and why do we need one?",
    "",
    "A message queue is a middleman that sits between two parts of a system. Instead of Service A calling Service B directly and waiting for a response, Service A drops a message on the queue and moves on. Service B picks it up whenever it's ready.",
    "",
    "**Why we need it:**",
    "- **Decoupling.** Service A and Service B don't need to know about each other. Either one can be updated, restarted, or replaced without breaking the other.",
    "- **Handling traffic spikes.** If 10,000 requests arrive in one second, the queue absorbs them. Workers process them at their own pace. Without a queue, Service B would crash or reject requests.",
    "- **Reliability.** If Service B goes down, messages wait in the queue. When it comes back, it picks up where it left off. No data is lost.",
    "",
    "**Example:** When you place an order on Amazon, the website responds immediately with \"order confirmed.\" Behind the scenes, the order event sits in a queue. Separate services pick it up to charge your card, notify the warehouse, send a confirmation email, and update your order history -- all asynchronously.",
    "",
    "---",
    "",
    "### Q2: What is the difference between a message queue and a message broker?",
    "",
    "- A **message queue** is a specific data structure -- an ordered list of messages waiting to be processed. Think of it like a single pipe: messages go in one end, come out the other.",
    "- A **message broker** is the entire server/system that manages queues, routes messages, handles connections, and ensures delivery. Think of it like a post office that contains many mailboxes (queues), sorting rules (routing), and delivery guarantees.",
    "",
    "RabbitMQ is a message broker. It contains many queues inside it. SQS is both -- AWS manages the broker, and you interact with individual queues.",
    "",
    "---",
    "",
    "### Q3: What is the difference between a queue and a topic?",
    "",
    "- A **queue** follows the point-to-point pattern. Each message goes to exactly one consumer. If 3 workers are listening on the same queue, each message is delivered to only one of them. Think of a to-do list where each task is assigned to one person.",
    "- A **topic** follows the publish/subscribe pattern. Each message goes to all subscribers. If 3 services subscribe to a topic, all 3 get a copy of every message. Think of a radio broadcast -- everyone tuned in hears the same thing.",
    "",
    "**When to use which:**",
    "- Queue: background jobs, task processing (send email, resize image).",
    "- Topic: event notifications where multiple services need to react (user signed up, order placed).",
    "",
    "---",
    "",
    "### Q4: What is the difference between synchronous and asynchronous communication?",
    "",
    "- **Synchronous:** Service A calls Service B and waits for a response. Like a phone call -- you speak, you wait for the other person to reply, then you continue.",
    "- **Asynchronous:** Service A sends a message and moves on without waiting. Like a text message -- you send it and do other things. The reply comes later.",
    "",
    "Message queues enable asynchronous communication. The producer doesn't wait for the consumer to process the message.",
    "",
    "**Trade-off:** Synchronous is simpler and gives you an immediate answer. Asynchronous is more resilient and scalable, but you have to deal with the fact that you don't know when (or if) the work is done unless you build a way to check.",
    "",
    "---",
    "",
    "### Q5: What is the difference between push and pull delivery?",
    "",
    "- **Push:** The broker sends messages to the consumer as soon as they arrive. The consumer doesn't ask for them -- they just show up. RabbitMQ does this by default.",
    "- **Pull:** The consumer asks the broker \"do you have any new messages?\" at regular intervals. Kafka works this way -- consumers fetch batches of messages.",
    "",
    "**Push** is better for low-latency scenarios (real-time dashboards). **Pull** is better for high-throughput scenarios because the consumer controls how fast it processes (backpressure).",
    "",
    "---",
    "",
    "### Q6: What is a Dead Letter Queue (DLQ)?",
    "",
    "A DLQ is a special queue where messages go when they can't be processed successfully. Think of it as the \"undeliverable mail\" bin at a post office.",
    "",
    "**When messages end up in a DLQ:**",
    "- The consumer tried to process the message 3 times (or whatever limit you set) and failed every time.",
    "- The message expired (TTL ran out before anyone processed it).",
    "- The message format is invalid and no consumer can parse it.",
    "",
    "**Why it matters:** Without a DLQ, a \"poison message\" (one that always fails) would be retried forever, blocking the queue. With a DLQ, the bad message is moved aside, the main queue keeps flowing, and an engineer can inspect the DLQ later to figure out what went wrong.",
    "",
    "---",
    "",
    "### Q7: What are producers, consumers, and brokers?",
    "",
    "- **Producer:** The part of your system that creates and sends messages. Example: the Order Service that publishes \"order placed\" events.",
    "- **Consumer:** The part that reads and processes messages. Example: the Email Service that reads \"order placed\" and sends a confirmation email.",
    "- **Broker:** The server in the middle that receives messages from producers, stores them, and delivers them to consumers. Example: RabbitMQ, Kafka, SQS.",
    "",
    "The producer and consumer never talk directly. The broker is always in between."
  ].join("\n"),

  "Delivery Guarantees and Reliability": [
    "## Delivery Guarantees and Reliability",
    "",
    "### Q8: What are the three delivery guarantees? Explain each.",
    "",
    "- **At-most-once:** The message is delivered zero or one time. The broker sends it and forgets. If the consumer crashes before processing, the message is lost. Fastest, but unreliable. Good for non-critical metrics where losing a few data points is acceptable.",
    "- **At-least-once:** The message is delivered one or more times. The broker keeps retrying until the consumer acknowledges. If the consumer processes the message but the ACK is lost (network glitch), the broker resends it, causing a duplicate. Reliable, but you may see duplicates.",
    "- **Exactly-once:** The message is delivered exactly one time. This is the hardest to achieve. It requires coordination between the producer, broker, and consumer (idempotency, transactions, deduplication). Most expensive in terms of performance. Used for critical things like financial transactions.",
    "",
    "**Follow-up question interviewers love:** \"Is exactly-once really possible?\" The honest answer is: true end-to-end exactly-once across different systems is extremely hard. Kafka achieves it within its own ecosystem using idempotent producers and transactional APIs, but the moment you write to an external database, you need idempotency on the consumer side too.",
    "",
    "---",
    "",
    "### Q9: What does \"idempotent\" mean and why does it matter for message queues?",
    "",
    "An operation is **idempotent** if doing it once or doing it ten times produces the same result.",
    "",
    "**Example:** \"Set the user's email to alice@example.com\" is idempotent -- doing it twice doesn't change anything. \"Add $10 to the user's balance\" is NOT idempotent -- doing it twice adds $20.",
    "",
    "**Why it matters:** With at-least-once delivery, consumers may receive the same message more than once. If your consumer is idempotent, duplicates are harmless. If not, you'll charge a customer twice, send two emails, or create duplicate records.",
    "",
    "**How to make consumers idempotent:**",
    "- Store a unique message ID in your database. Before processing, check if you've seen this ID before. If yes, skip it.",
    "- Use database upserts (INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING) instead of plain inserts.",
    "- Design operations as \"set to X\" instead of \"add X.\"",
    "",
    "---",
    "",
    "### Q10: What is message acknowledgment (ACK/NACK)?",
    "",
    "- **ACK (Acknowledge):** The consumer tells the broker \"I'm done with this message, you can delete it.\" The broker removes it from the queue.",
    "- **NACK (Negative Acknowledge):** The consumer tells the broker \"I couldn't process this, put it back.\" The broker requeues the message for another attempt.",
    "- **Auto-ACK:** The broker considers the message delivered the moment it sends it. If the consumer crashes after receiving but before processing, the message is gone. Fast, but risky.",
    "- **Manual ACK:** The consumer explicitly sends the ACK after successful processing. Safer, because the message stays in the queue until it's definitely handled.",
    "",
    "**Interview tip:** Always mention that production systems should use manual ACK, because auto-ACK risks losing messages on consumer crashes.",
    "",
    "---",
    "",
    "### Q11: What is a poison message and how do you handle it?",
    "",
    "A poison message is a message that causes the consumer to fail every time it tries to process it. Maybe the JSON is malformed, a required field is missing, or the message triggers a bug in the consumer's code.",
    "",
    "**The danger:** Without protection, the broker keeps redelivering the poison message. The consumer keeps crashing. The queue is stuck -- no other messages behind it get processed.",
    "",
    "**How to handle it:**",
    "1. Set a **max retry count** (e.g., 3 attempts).",
    "2. After the max, move the message to a **Dead Letter Queue**.",
    "3. Alert the team so someone can inspect the DLQ.",
    "4. The main queue continues processing other messages normally.",
    "",
    "---",
    "",
    "### Q12: What is backpressure and how do you handle it?",
    "",
    "Backpressure happens when producers send messages faster than consumers can process them. The queue grows, memory fills up, and eventually the broker crashes or starts rejecting messages.",
    "",
    "**How to handle it:**",
    "- **Prefetch limit (RabbitMQ):** Tell the broker \"only send me 10 messages at a time.\" The broker won't send message #11 until one of the first 10 is ACKed.",
    "- **Consumer lag monitoring (Kafka):** Track how far behind each consumer group is. If lag grows above a threshold, alert the team to add more consumers.",
    "- **Queue length limits:** Set a max queue size. When reached, either reject new messages (tell the producer to slow down), drop the oldest messages, or route overflow to a different queue.",
    "- **Scale consumers:** Add more worker instances to drain the queue faster."
  ].join("\n"),

  "RabbitMQ": [
    "## RabbitMQ",
    "",
    "### Q13: What is an exchange in RabbitMQ? Name all four types.",
    "",
    "An exchange is a routing layer. Producers send messages to an exchange (never directly to a queue). The exchange looks at the message's routing key and decides which queue(s) should receive it based on bindings.",
    "",
    "**Four types:**",
    "1. **Direct:** Routes to queues where the binding key exactly matches the routing key. Routing key `order.created` goes only to queues bound to `order.created`.",
    "2. **Fanout:** Ignores the routing key entirely. Copies the message to every bound queue. Used for broadcasting.",
    "3. **Topic:** Pattern matching. Routing key `order.created` matches bindings like `order.*` (one word wildcard) and `#` (everything). Most flexible.",
    "4. **Headers:** Routes based on message header key-value pairs instead of routing key. Useful when routing depends on multiple attributes.",
    "",
    "---",
    "",
    "### Q14: What are quorum queues in RabbitMQ?",
    "",
    "Quorum queues are RabbitMQ's modern high-availability queue type. They use the **Raft consensus algorithm** to replicate messages across multiple nodes.",
    "",
    "**How they work:**",
    "- One node is the **leader** (accepts writes).",
    "- Other nodes are the **followers** (replicate data from the leader).",
    "- A message is only confirmed to the producer once a **majority** (quorum) of nodes have stored it.",
    "- If the leader dies, a follower is automatically elected as the new leader.",
    "",
    "**Why they matter:** Classic mirrored queues (the old HA approach) had split-brain problems and data loss edge cases. Quorum queues solve these with proper consensus, at the cost of slightly higher latency (because the majority must confirm before ACKing the producer).",
    "",
    "---",
    "",
    "### Q15: How does RabbitMQ handle message persistence?",
    "",
    "Two things must be true for a message to survive a broker restart:",
    "1. The **queue** must be declared as `durable: true`.",
    "2. The **message** must be published with `delivery_mode: 2` (persistent).",
    "",
    "If either is missing, the message lives only in memory and is lost on restart. Even with both set, there's a small window where a message is received but not yet flushed to disk. Quorum queues close this gap by requiring majority confirmation."
  ].join("\n"),

  "Apache Kafka": [
    "## Apache Kafka",
    "",
    "### Q16: What is a partition in Kafka and why does it matter?",
    "",
    "A partition is a slice of a topic. Each topic is divided into one or more partitions, and each partition is an ordered, append-only log stored on a broker.",
    "",
    "**Why it matters:**",
    "- **Parallelism.** Each partition can be consumed by a different consumer. 10 partitions = up to 10 consumers reading in parallel.",
    "- **Ordering.** Messages within a single partition are strictly ordered. Messages across partitions have no ordering guarantee. If order matters for a specific entity (e.g., all events for customer #42), use the entity ID as the partition key so they all land in the same partition.",
    "- **Scalability.** More partitions = more throughput. But too many partitions increases metadata overhead and rebalancing time.",
    "",
    "**Common interview follow-up:** \"Can you decrease the number of partitions?\" No. Once created, partitions can only be increased, never decreased. Choose wisely at topic creation.",
    "",
    "---",
    "",
    "### Q17: What is a consumer group in Kafka?",
    "",
    "A consumer group is a set of consumers that cooperate to read from a topic. Each partition is assigned to exactly one consumer in the group.",
    "",
    "**Example:** Topic with 3 partitions, consumer group with 2 consumers:",
    "- Consumer A reads partitions 0 and 1.",
    "- Consumer B reads partition 2.",
    "",
    "If Consumer A dies, its partitions are reassigned to Consumer B (rebalancing). If a third consumer joins, partitions are redistributed: one each.",
    "",
    "**Key rule:** You can never have more active consumers than partitions in a single group. If you have 3 partitions and 5 consumers, 2 consumers will sit idle.",
    "",
    "**Multiple groups:** Different consumer groups read the same topic independently. The analytics group and the search-indexer group each have their own offsets and don't interfere with each other.",
    "",
    "---",
    "",
    "### Q18: What is an offset in Kafka?",
    "",
    "An offset is a sequential number that identifies a message's position within a partition. The first message is offset 0, the next is 1, then 2, and so on.",
    "",
    "**How consumers use it:**",
    "- A consumer reads messages starting from its last **committed offset**.",
    "- After processing, it **commits** the new offset (\"I've processed up to offset 42\").",
    "- If it crashes and restarts, it resumes from the last committed offset (43).",
    "",
    "**Why it matters:**",
    "- **Replay:** Reset the offset to 0 and reprocess everything.",
    "- **Monitoring:** The difference between the latest offset and the committed offset is **consumer lag** -- how far behind the consumer is.",
    "",
    "---",
    "",
    "### Q19: What is log compaction in Kafka?",
    "",
    "Log compaction keeps only the **latest value for each key** in a topic, deleting older entries for the same key.",
    "",
    "**Example:** A topic tracking user addresses:",
    "- Offset 0: key=user-1, value=\"123 Main St\"",
    "- Offset 1: key=user-2, value=\"456 Oak Ave\"",
    "- Offset 2: key=user-1, value=\"789 Pine Rd\" (user-1 moved)",
    "",
    "After compaction: offset 0 is deleted (superseded by offset 2). Offsets 1 and 2 remain. The topic now acts like a database table -- one row per user with the latest address.",
    "",
    "**Use cases:** CDC (change data capture), configuration distribution, maintaining materialized views.",
    "",
    "---",
    "",
    "### Q20: What is the difference between Kafka and RabbitMQ?",
    "",
    "| Aspect | RabbitMQ | Kafka |",
    "|---|---|---|",
    "| **Model** | Message broker with queues | Distributed event log |",
    "| **After consumption** | Message is deleted | Message stays (retained for days/weeks) |",
    "| **Replay** | Not possible | Yes, seek to any offset |",
    "| **Routing** | Rich (4 exchange types) | None (topic + partition only) |",
    "| **Throughput** | ~50K msg/s | Millions msg/s |",
    "| **Ordering** | Per-queue FIFO | Per-partition FIFO |",
    "| **Best for** | Task queues, RPC, complex routing | Event streaming, log aggregation, event sourcing |",
    "| **Consumer model** | Broker pushes to consumers | Consumers pull from brokers |",
    "",
    "**Short answer for interviews:** \"RabbitMQ is a smart broker with dumb consumers (the broker decides where messages go). Kafka is a dumb broker with smart consumers (the broker just stores the log, consumers decide where to read from).\"",
    "",
    "---",
    "",
    "### Q21: How does Kafka achieve high throughput?",
    "",
    "Kafka is fast because of several design choices that work together:",
    "",
    "1. **Sequential disk I/O.** Messages are appended to the end of a file. Sequential writes are extremely fast -- even faster than random reads from RAM on modern disks.",
    "2. **Zero-copy transfer.** When a consumer reads, the OS sends data directly from the disk page cache to the network socket without copying it through application memory. This avoids two expensive memory copies.",
    "3. **Batching.** Producers batch multiple messages into a single network request. Consumers fetch in batches too. This amortizes the network overhead across many messages.",
    "4. **Partitioning.** Multiple partitions spread the load across multiple brokers and disks, allowing parallel writes and reads.",
    "5. **Compression.** Producers can compress batches (gzip, snappy, lz4, zstd) before sending, reducing network bandwidth."
  ].join("\n"),

  "AWS SQS / SNS": [
    "## AWS SQS / SNS",
    "",
    "### Q22: What is the difference between SQS Standard and SQS FIFO?",
    "",
    "| Feature | Standard | FIFO |",
    "|---|---|---|",
    "| **Throughput** | Nearly unlimited | ~3,000 msg/s (with batching) |",
    "| **Ordering** | Best-effort (may be out of order) | Strict FIFO within a message group |",
    "| **Delivery** | At-least-once (duplicates possible) | Exactly-once (within dedup window) |",
    "| **Use case** | High-volume work queues | Order-sensitive workflows |",
    "",
    "**When to use Standard:** You need high throughput and can handle occasional duplicates or out-of-order messages (e.g., sending notification emails -- getting two is annoying but not catastrophic).",
    "",
    "**When to use FIFO:** Order matters and duplicates would cause real problems (e.g., processing bank transactions -- \"deposit then withdraw\" must not become \"withdraw then deposit\").",
    "",
    "---",
    "",
    "### Q23: What is a visibility timeout in SQS?",
    "",
    "When a consumer receives a message from SQS, the message doesn't disappear -- it becomes **invisible** to other consumers for a period (default 30 seconds). This is the visibility timeout.",
    "",
    "**What happens next:**",
    "- If the consumer finishes processing and **deletes** the message before the timeout, it's gone permanently.",
    "- If the consumer crashes or the timeout expires before deletion, the message **becomes visible again** and another consumer can pick it up.",
    "",
    "**Why it matters:** This is how SQS handles retries without you writing any retry logic. If processing fails, the message automatically gets another chance.",
    "",
    "**Tuning tip:** Set the timeout longer than your expected processing time. If your job takes 2 minutes but the timeout is 30 seconds, the message will become visible again while the first consumer is still working on it, causing duplicate processing.",
    "",
    "---",
    "",
    "### Q24: What is the difference between SQS and SNS?",
    "",
    "- **SQS (Simple Queue Service)** is a queue. One message goes to one consumer (point-to-point). Think of it as a to-do list -- each item is handled by one worker.",
    "- **SNS (Simple Notification Service)** is a pub/sub system. One message goes to all subscribers (fan-out). Think of it as a newsletter -- every subscriber gets a copy.",
    "",
    "**Used together:** SNS for fan-out, SQS for reliable processing. Publish to an SNS topic, which copies the message to 5 SQS queues, each consumed by a different service. This is the most common AWS messaging pattern."
  ].join("\n"),

  "System Design and Architecture": [
    "## System Design and Architecture",
    "",
    "### Q25: How would you design a notification system using message queues?",
    "",
    "**Requirements:** When an event happens (new follower, new message, new like), send notifications through multiple channels (push notification, email, SMS, in-app).",
    "",
    "**Design:**",
    "",
    "1. **Event producers** (the Follow Service, Message Service, Like Service) publish events to a topic/exchange.",
    "2. **Notification Router** consumes events and decides which channels to use based on user preferences (user A wants push + email, user B wants SMS only).",
    "3. For each channel, it publishes a job to a channel-specific queue:",
    "   - `push-notification-queue`",
    "   - `email-queue`",
    "   - `sms-queue`",
    "4. **Channel workers** consume from their respective queues and deliver the notification.",
    "5. Each queue has a **DLQ** for failed deliveries.",
    "6. A **retry policy** with exponential backoff handles transient failures (email server temporarily down).",
    "",
    "**Why a queue?** If the email server is slow or down, the queue buffers thousands of pending emails. Users don't see errors. When the server recovers, emails are sent in order.",
    "",
    "---",
    "",
    "### Q26: How would you handle exactly-once processing in a payment system?",
    "",
    "**The problem:** A payment event arrives (\"charge customer $50\"). If it's processed twice, the customer is charged $100. If it's lost, the customer isn't charged at all.",
    "",
    "**Solution: idempotent consumer with a deduplication store.**",
    "",
    "1. Each payment message has a unique `paymentId`.",
    "2. Before processing, the consumer checks a database: \"Have I already processed paymentId=abc123?\"",
    "3. If yes, skip it (duplicate).",
    "4. If no, process the payment AND write `paymentId=abc123` to the deduplication table in the **same database transaction**.",
    "5. ACK the message.",
    "",
    "**Why this works:** Even if the message is delivered twice (network retry, consumer restart), the second attempt sees the ID in the deduplication table and does nothing. The database transaction ensures the payment and the deduplication record are saved atomically -- either both succeed or both fail.",
    "",
    "---",
    "",
    "### Q27: How would you design a system that processes 1 million events per second?",
    "",
    "**Answer: Kafka with horizontal scaling.**",
    "",
    "1. Create a topic with many partitions (e.g., 100-200).",
    "2. Run a cluster of Kafka brokers (e.g., 10-20) spread across multiple machines. Partitions are distributed across brokers.",
    "3. Producers batch messages and use compression (lz4 or zstd).",
    "4. Run a consumer group with as many consumers as partitions (e.g., 100-200 consumer instances).",
    "5. Each consumer processes its assigned partitions independently.",
    "6. Monitor consumer lag to detect if consumers are falling behind.",
    "7. If throughput isn't enough, add more partitions and consumers.",
    "",
    "**Why Kafka:** Sequential disk writes + zero-copy reads + batching + partitioning = designed from the ground up for this scale. RabbitMQ would struggle past ~50K msg/s per node. SQS Standard could handle the throughput but can't guarantee ordering or support replay.",
    "",
    "---",
    "",
    "### Q28: What is the Transactional Outbox pattern?",
    "",
    "**The problem:** Your service needs to update a database AND publish a message to a queue. If you do them as two separate operations, either one can fail:",
    "- DB write succeeds, message publish fails = the queue is out of sync.",
    "- Message publish succeeds, DB write fails = the queue has events for things that didn't happen.",
    "",
    "**The solution:** Write the message to an **outbox table** in the same database, inside the same transaction as the business data.",
    "",
    "1. In one transaction: INSERT the order into the `orders` table AND INSERT the event into the `outbox` table.",
    "2. A separate background process (or CDC tool like Debezium) reads the outbox table and publishes messages to the queue.",
    "3. After publishing, mark the outbox row as \"sent.\"",
    "",
    "**Why it works:** The database transaction guarantees both writes succeed or both fail. The outbox reader is idempotent -- if it crashes and retries, it just re-publishes (and the consumer handles duplicates via idempotency).",
    "",
    "---",
    "",
    "### Q29: What is the Saga pattern and how do message queues fit in?",
    "",
    "**The problem:** A business operation spans multiple services (order service, payment service, inventory service). You can't use a single database transaction because each service has its own database.",
    "",
    "**The Saga pattern** breaks the operation into a chain of local transactions, coordinated through messages:",
    "",
    "1. Order Service creates order (status: PENDING) and publishes `OrderCreated`.",
    "2. Payment Service hears `OrderCreated`, charges the customer, publishes `PaymentSucceeded`.",
    "3. Inventory Service hears `PaymentSucceeded`, reserves stock, publishes `StockReserved`.",
    "4. Order Service hears `StockReserved`, marks order as CONFIRMED.",
    "",
    "**If something fails (e.g., Payment fails):**",
    "- Payment Service publishes `PaymentFailed`.",
    "- Order Service hears it and cancels the order (compensating transaction).",
    "",
    "**Two flavors:**",
    "- **Choreography:** Each service listens for events and decides what to do. No coordinator. Simple but hard to trace.",
    "- **Orchestration:** A central \"saga orchestrator\" tells each service what to do and handles failures. More control but adds a single point of coordination."
  ].join("\n"),

  "Troubleshooting and Production Issues": [
    "## Troubleshooting and Production Issues",
    "",
    "### Q30: Messages are piling up in the queue and consumers can't keep up. What do you do?",
    "",
    "This is a **consumer lag** problem. Walk through this checklist:",
    "",
    "1. **Check consumer health.** Are consumers running? Are they throwing errors? Check logs first.",
    "2. **Check processing time.** Is each message taking too long? Maybe a downstream service (database, API) is slow. Fix the bottleneck.",
    "3. **Scale horizontally.** Add more consumer instances. In Kafka, you can add consumers up to the number of partitions. In RabbitMQ/SQS, just add more workers.",
    "4. **Increase prefetch/batch size.** If consumers spend more time waiting for messages than processing them, fetch more messages per round trip.",
    "5. **Check for poison messages.** One bad message retrying infinitely can block the queue. Make sure you have a DLQ and max retry limit.",
    "6. **Temporarily increase resources.** Give consumers more CPU/memory if they're resource-constrained.",
    "7. **Back-pressure the producer.** If nothing else works, slow down the producer (rate limiting) to prevent the queue from growing further while you fix the root cause.",
    "",
    "---",
    "",
    "### Q31: A consumer processed a message but the ACK was lost. What happens?",
    "",
    "The broker thinks the message was never processed (because it never received the ACK). It will **redeliver** the message to the same or a different consumer.",
    "",
    "**Result:** The message is processed twice.",
    "",
    "**How to prevent problems from this:**",
    "- Make your consumer **idempotent** (processing the same message twice has the same effect as processing it once).",
    "- Use a deduplication store: before processing, check if you've already handled this message ID.",
    "",
    "This is a fundamental reality of distributed systems: you can't have both \"never lose a message\" and \"never process a duplicate\" without significant coordination (idempotent consumers, transactional exactly-once, etc.).",
    "",
    "---",
    "",
    "### Q32: How do you monitor a message queue in production?",
    "",
    "**Key metrics to watch:**",
    "",
    "| Metric | What it tells you | Alert when |",
    "|---|---|---|",
    "| **Queue depth / consumer lag** | How many messages are waiting | Growing steadily (consumers can't keep up) |",
    "| **Processing rate** | Messages consumed per second | Drops significantly |",
    "| **Error rate** | Messages that fail processing | Spikes |",
    "| **DLQ size** | Messages that permanently failed | Any messages appear |",
    "| **Consumer count** | Number of active consumers | Drops below expected |",
    "| **Message age** | How long the oldest message has been waiting | Exceeds your SLA |",
    "| **Broker disk / memory** | Resource usage of the broker | Approaching limits |",
    "",
    "**Tools:**",
    "- RabbitMQ: Built-in Management UI (port 15672), Prometheus exporter.",
    "- Kafka: Burrow (consumer lag), Cruise Control (cluster balancing), Prometheus + Grafana.",
    "- SQS: CloudWatch metrics (ApproximateNumberOfMessagesVisible, ApproximateAgeOfOldestMessage).",
    "",
    "---",
    "",
    "### Q33: Your Kafka consumer group is constantly rebalancing. What's wrong?",
    "",
    "Rebalancing happens when consumers join or leave a group. If it's happening constantly, consumers are likely **timing out** and being kicked from the group, then rejoining, causing another rebalance.",
    "",
    "**Common causes:**",
    "1. **Processing takes too long.** The consumer doesn't call `poll()` within `max.poll.interval.ms` (default 5 minutes). Kafka thinks it's dead and triggers rebalance. Fix: increase the interval or reduce `max.poll.records` so each poll cycle processes fewer messages.",
    "2. **GC pauses.** Long Java garbage collection pauses can cause the consumer to miss heartbeats (`session.timeout.ms`, default 45s). Fix: tune JVM GC settings.",
    "3. **Consumer crashes and restarts in a loop.** A bug causes the consumer to crash, restart, rejoin, crash again. Fix: check logs for the underlying error.",
    "4. **Network issues.** Unstable network between consumers and brokers causes heartbeat failures. Fix: check network health.",
    "",
    "**Impact of constant rebalancing:** During rebalance, all consumers in the group **stop processing**. If rebalancing takes 30 seconds and happens every minute, your consumers are idle half the time.",
    "",
    "---",
    "",
    "### Q34: How do you guarantee message ordering?",
    "",
    "**The hard truth:** Guaranteeing global order across all messages is expensive and limits throughput. Most systems guarantee order within a subset.",
    "",
    "**By queue type:**",
    "- **RabbitMQ:** Messages in a single queue are FIFO. But if you have multiple consumers on one queue, messages may be processed out of order (consumer A takes message 1, consumer B takes message 2, B finishes first). Solution: use a single consumer per queue, or design for out-of-order processing.",
    "- **Kafka:** Messages within a single partition are strictly ordered. Use a partition key (e.g., customer ID) to ensure all events for the same entity go to the same partition.",
    "- **SQS Standard:** No ordering guarantee at all. Use SQS FIFO with message group IDs for ordering.",
    "- **SQS FIFO:** Strict ordering within a message group. Different groups are processed independently.",
    "",
    "**Interview tip:** Don't say \"Kafka guarantees ordering.\" Say \"Kafka guarantees ordering within a partition.\" This shows you understand the nuance.",
    "",
    "---",
    "",
    "### Q35: How do you handle schema evolution in message queues?",
    "",
    "**The problem:** The producer changes the message format (adds a new field, removes an old one, changes a type). Consumers that expect the old format break.",
    "",
    "**Solutions:**",
    "1. **Schema registry (Kafka).** Use a schema registry (Confluent Schema Registry) that stores Avro/Protobuf/JSON schemas. Producers and consumers validate against the registry. The registry enforces compatibility rules (backward, forward, full).",
    "2. **Backward compatibility.** New fields must have default values. Old consumers ignore unknown fields. Old messages can be read by new consumers (missing fields use defaults).",
    "3. **Versioning.** Include a `version` field in the message. Consumers check the version and handle different formats.",
    "4. **Contract testing.** Test that producer output matches consumer expectations before deployment.",
    "",
    "**Best practice:** Use Avro or Protobuf (not raw JSON) with a schema registry. They enforce structure and handle evolution gracefully."
  ].join("\n")
};

/**
 * Spring Framework — Questions & Answers
 *
 * Keyed by the exact section heading from Spring_Interview_Questions_Study_Guide.md.
 */
var SPRING_QUESTION_SECTIONS = {
  "Spring Core & IoC": [
    "## Spring Core & IoC",
    "",
    "### 1. What is Spring Framework?",
    "",
    "Spring is a lightweight, open-source Java application framework created by **Rod Johnson in 2003** as an alternative to the heavyweight J2EE model. It provides comprehensive infrastructure support for developing enterprise Java applications through dependency injection, aspect-oriented programming, and a modular architecture. The framework enables developers to build loosely coupled, testable, and maintainable applications while reducing boilerplate code.",
    "",
    "### 2. What are the benefits of using Spring?",
    "",
    "- **Lightweight container** — minimal overhead compared to traditional Java EE servers",
    "- **Loose coupling** through Dependency Injection, making components independently testable",
    "- **Modular design** — use only the modules you need (Core, MVC, Data, Security, etc.)",
    "- **AOP support** for separating cross-cutting concerns (logging, transactions, security)",
    "- **Declarative transaction management** without requiring a full application server",
    "- **Built-in integration** with JDBC, JPA/Hibernate, JMS, REST, and other technologies",
    "- **Extensive testing support** with `@SpringBootTest`, mock objects, and `TestContext` framework",
    "- **Large ecosystem** and active community with well-maintained documentation",
    "",
    "### 3. What are the different modules in Spring?",
    "",
    "| Module Group | Key Modules | Purpose |",
    "|---|---|---|",
    "| **Core Container** | Core, Beans, Context, SpEL | IoC container, DI, application context, expression language |",
    "| **Data Access** | JDBC, ORM, Transaction, JMS | Database access, ORM integration, declarative transactions |",
    "| **Web** | Web MVC, WebFlux, WebSocket | Servlet-based and reactive web frameworks |",
    "| **AOP & Instrumentation** | AOP, Aspects, Instrumentation | Cross-cutting concerns, AspectJ integration |",
    "| **Security** | Spring Security | Authentication, authorization, CSRF protection |",
    "| **Test** | Spring Test | Unit and integration testing support |",
    "",
    "The **Core Container** is the foundation — it provides the IoC container (`BeanFactory`, `ApplicationContext`) that all other modules build upon.",
    "",
    "### 4. What is Dependency Injection?",
    "",
    "Dependency Injection (DI) is a design pattern where an object's dependencies are provided by an external entity (the container) rather than created by the object itself. Instead of a class calling `new` on its dependencies, the Spring container injects them at runtime.",
    "",
    "```java",
    "// Without DI — tight coupling",
    "public class OrderService {",
    "    private PaymentService paymentService = new PaymentService();",
    "}",
    "",
    "// With DI — loose coupling",
    "public class OrderService {",
    "    private final PaymentService paymentService;",
    "",
    "    public OrderService(PaymentService paymentService) {",
    "        this.paymentService = paymentService;",
    "    }",
    "}",
    "```",
    "",
    "DI promotes loose coupling, easier testing (swap real implementations with mocks), and better separation of concerns.",
    "",
    "### 5. What is Inversion of Control?",
    "",
    "Inversion of Control (IoC) is a broader principle where the control of object creation and lifecycle is transferred from the application code to a framework or container. DI is the most common implementation of IoC. In traditional programming, your code calls library functions; with IoC, the framework calls your code (the \"Hollywood Principle\": don't call us, we'll call you).",
    "",
    "In Spring, the **IoC container** (`ApplicationContext`) manages bean creation, wiring, configuration, and destruction — the developer defines *what* the dependencies are, and the container handles *how* and *when* they are provided.",
    "",
    "### 6. What are the types of Dependency Injection?",
    "",
    "**Constructor Injection** (recommended) — dependencies provided via constructor arguments. Guarantees immutability and that the bean is fully initialized.",
    "",
    "```java",
    "@Service",
    "public class OrderService {",
    "    private final PaymentService paymentService;",
    "",
    "    public OrderService(PaymentService paymentService) {",
    "        this.paymentService = paymentService;",
    "    }",
    "}",
    "```",
    "",
    "**Setter Injection** — dependencies provided via setter methods. Suitable for optional dependencies.",
    "",
    "```java",
    "@Service",
    "public class OrderService {",
    "    private PaymentService paymentService;",
    "",
    "    @Autowired",
    "    public void setPaymentService(PaymentService paymentService) {",
    "        this.paymentService = paymentService;",
    "    }",
    "}",
    "```",
    "",
    "**Field Injection** — dependencies injected directly into fields. Convenient but discouraged in production because it hides dependencies, makes testing harder, and prevents immutability.",
    "",
    "```java",
    "@Service",
    "public class OrderService {",
    "    @Autowired",
    "    private PaymentService paymentService; // avoid in production code",
    "}",
    "```",
    "",
    "### 7. What is a Spring Bean?",
    "",
    "A Spring Bean is any object that is instantiated, configured, and managed by the Spring IoC container. Beans are defined through annotations (`@Component`, `@Service`, `@Bean`, etc.) or XML configuration. The container is responsible for their full lifecycle — from creation to destruction. Any POJO can become a Spring Bean when registered with the container.",
    "",
    "### 8. What are the different scopes of Spring Bean?",
    "",
    "| Scope | Description | When Destroyed |",
    "|---|---|---|",
    "| **singleton** | One instance per Spring container (default) | Container shutdown |",
    "| **prototype** | New instance every time the bean is requested | Not managed by container |",
    "| **request** | One instance per HTTP request (web only) | Request completes |",
    "| **session** | One instance per HTTP session (web only) | Session expires |",
    "| **application** | One instance per `ServletContext` (web only) | Application shutdown |",
    "| **websocket** | One instance per WebSocket session (web only) | WebSocket closes |",
    "",
    "### 9. What is the default scope of a Spring Bean?",
    "",
    "The default scope is **singleton**. The Spring container creates exactly one instance of the bean and caches it — every subsequent injection or lookup returns the same object. This is different from the GoF Singleton pattern because singleton scope is per-container, not per-JVM.",
    "",
    "### 10. What is the Bean lifecycle in Spring?",
    "",
    "1. **Instantiation** — container creates the bean instance",
    "2. **Property Population** — dependencies are injected",
    "3. **`BeanNameAware.setBeanName()`** — bean receives its name",
    "4. **`BeanFactoryAware.setBeanFactory()`** — bean receives the factory reference",
    "5. **`ApplicationContextAware.setApplicationContext()`** — bean receives the context",
    "6. **`BeanPostProcessor.postProcessBeforeInitialization()`** — pre-init processing",
    "7. **`@PostConstruct`** — annotated method called",
    "8. **`InitializingBean.afterPropertiesSet()`** — callback interface",
    "9. **Custom `init-method`** — XML-configured initialization method",
    "10. **`BeanPostProcessor.postProcessAfterInitialization()`** — post-init processing",
    "11. **Bean is ready for use**",
    "12. **`@PreDestroy`** — annotated method called (on shutdown)",
    "13. **`DisposableBean.destroy()`** — callback interface",
    "14. **Custom `destroy-method`** — XML-configured destruction method",
    "",
    "### 11. What is the difference between BeanFactory and ApplicationContext?",
    "",
    "| Feature | BeanFactory | ApplicationContext |",
    "|---|---|---|",
    "| **Bean Loading** | Lazy (on first request) | Eager (at startup) |",
    "| **Event Publishing** | No | Yes (`ApplicationEvent`) |",
    "| **Internationalization** | No | Yes (`MessageSource`) |",
    "| **AOP Integration** | Manual | Automatic |",
    "| **`BeanPostProcessor` registration** | Manual | Automatic |",
    "| **Annotation Support** | Limited | Full |",
    "| **Use Case** | Resource-constrained environments | Enterprise applications |",
    "",
    "`ApplicationContext` extends `BeanFactory` and is the preferred choice for virtually all applications.",
    "",
    "### 12. What is Autowiring in Spring?",
    "",
    "Autowiring allows the Spring container to automatically resolve and inject bean dependencies without explicit configuration. Instead of manually wiring every dependency via XML or `@Bean` methods, the container infers which beans to inject based on type, name, or constructor signatures.",
    "",
    "### 13. What are the different modes of Autowiring?",
    "",
    "| Mode | Description |",
    "|---|---|",
    "| **no** | Default in XML. No autowiring; dependencies must be explicitly configured. |",
    "| **byName** | Matches bean property name to a bean id/name in the container. |",
    "| **byType** | Matches by the type of the property. Throws exception if multiple candidates exist. |",
    "| **constructor** | Similar to byType but applies to constructor arguments. |",
    "| **autodetect** | (Deprecated in Spring 3+) Tried constructor first, then byType. |",
    "",
    "In annotation-based configuration, `@Autowired` uses **byType** by default, falling back to **byName** when combined with `@Qualifier`.",
    "",
    "### 14. What is the use of @Autowired annotation?",
    "",
    "`@Autowired` tells the Spring container to automatically inject a matching bean dependency. It can be placed on constructors, setters, or fields. By default it resolves **by type**; if multiple beans of the same type exist, use `@Qualifier` to disambiguate. Set `required = false` for optional dependencies. Since Spring 4.3, `@Autowired` is optional on a class with a single constructor.",
    "",
    "### 15. What is the difference between constructor injection and setter injection?",
    "",
    "| Aspect | Constructor Injection | Setter Injection |",
    "|---|---|---|",
    "| **Immutability** | Fields can be `final` | Fields must be mutable |",
    "| **Required Dependencies** | Enforced at compile time | Can be `null` at runtime |",
    "| **Circular Dependencies** | Detected at startup (fails fast) | Resolved via proxy/lazy |",
    "| **Readability** | All deps visible in constructor | Scattered across setters |",
    "| **Testing** | Easy — pass mocks via constructor | Requires calling setters |",
    "| **Use Case** | Mandatory dependencies | Optional/changeable dependencies |",
    "",
    "**Best practice**: Use constructor injection for mandatory dependencies and setter injection only for truly optional ones.",
    "",
    "### 16. How can you inject a Java Collection in Spring?",
    "",
    "Spring supports injecting `List`, `Set`, `Map`, and `Properties` collections. Using `@Autowired`, Spring injects all beans of a matching type:",
    "",
    "```java",
    "@Service",
    "public class NotificationService {",
    "    private final List<NotificationChannel> channels;",
    "",
    "    @Autowired",
    "    public NotificationService(List<NotificationChannel> channels) {",
    "        this.channels = channels; // all NotificationChannel beans",
    "    }",
    "}",
    "```",
    "",
    "Collections can also be injected via XML `<list>`, `<set>`, `<map>`, `<props>` elements, or from property files using `@Value` with SpEL.",
    "",
    "### 17. What is the role of @Component, @Repository, @Service, and @Controller annotations?",
    "",
    "All four are **stereotype annotations** detected via component scanning. They share the same DI behavior but carry semantic meaning:",
    "",
    "| Annotation | Layer | Extra Behavior |",
    "|---|---|---|",
    "| `@Component` | Generic | Base annotation — no extra behavior |",
    "| `@Repository` | Data Access | **Automatic exception translation** (JDBC/JPA exceptions → Spring's `DataAccessException`) |",
    "| `@Service` | Business Logic | No extra behavior; marks service-layer intent |",
    "| `@Controller` | Web / Presentation | Marks as MVC controller; enables `@RequestMapping` handling |",
    "",
    "### 18. What is the difference between @Component and @Bean?",
    "",
    "| Aspect | @Component | @Bean |",
    "|---|---|---|",
    "| **Target** | Class-level | Method-level (inside `@Configuration`) |",
    "| **Detection** | Auto-detected via component scanning | Explicitly declared |",
    "| **Use Case** | Your own classes | Third-party library classes you can't annotate |",
    "| **Customization** | Limited | Full programmatic control over instantiation |",
    "",
    "Use `@Component` for your own classes and `@Bean` for third-party classes (e.g., `RestTemplate`, `ObjectMapper`).",
    "",
    "### 19. How do you define a Bean in Spring using XML?",
    "",
    "```xml",
    "<beans xmlns=\"http://www.springframework.org/schema/beans\" ...>",
    "    <bean id=\"paymentService\" class=\"com.example.PaymentService\"/>",
    "",
    "    <bean id=\"orderService\" class=\"com.example.OrderService\">",
    "        <constructor-arg ref=\"paymentService\"/>",
    "        <property name=\"maxRetries\" value=\"3\"/>",
    "    </bean>",
    "</beans>",
    "```",
    "",
    "XML configuration is largely replaced by Java-based config in modern Spring but is still used in legacy projects.",
    "",
    "### 20. How do you define a Bean using Java-based configuration?",
    "",
    "```java",
    "@Configuration",
    "public class AppConfig {",
    "",
    "    @Bean",
    "    public PaymentService paymentService() {",
    "        return new PaymentService();",
    "    }",
    "",
    "    @Bean",
    "    public OrderService orderService() {",
    "        return new OrderService(paymentService());",
    "    }",
    "}",
    "```",
    "",
    "This is the preferred modern approach — type-safe, refactor-friendly, and supports IDE navigation.",
    "",
    "### 21. What is the use of @Configuration and @Bean annotations?",
    "",
    "`@Configuration` marks a class as a source of bean definitions. Spring creates a **CGLIB proxy** of the class so that calls to `@Bean` methods return the existing singleton instance instead of creating a new object. This guarantees singleton semantics for inter-bean references.",
    "",
    "`@Bean` marks a method whose return value is registered as a bean. Without `@Configuration` (using `@Component` + `@Bean` — \"lite mode\"), inter-bean method calls create new instances each time because no CGLIB proxy is applied.",
    "",
    "### 22. What is the use of @Qualifier annotation?",
    "",
    "`@Qualifier` resolves ambiguity when multiple beans of the same type exist by specifying which bean to inject by name:",
    "",
    "```java",
    "@Service",
    "public class AlertService {",
    "    @Autowired",
    "    public AlertService(@Qualifier(\"smsSender\") NotificationSender sender) {",
    "        this.sender = sender;",
    "    }",
    "}",
    "```",
    "",
    "Without `@Qualifier`, Spring throws `NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException`.",
    "",
    "### 23. What is the use of @Primary annotation?",
    "",
    "`@Primary` designates a bean as the **default candidate** when multiple beans of the same type exist and no `@Qualifier` is specified. `@Qualifier` takes precedence over `@Primary` when both are present.",
    "",
    "### 24. What is the difference between @PostConstruct and @PreDestroy?",
    "",
    "- **`@PostConstruct`** — called **after** dependency injection is complete. Used for initialization logic that depends on injected fields.",
    "- **`@PreDestroy`** — called **before** the container destroys the bean. Used for cleanup (closing connections, releasing resources).",
    "",
    "Both are JSR-250 annotations. `@PreDestroy` does **not** apply to prototype-scoped beans.",
    "",
    "### 25. What is the use of init-method and destroy-method in XML configuration?",
    "",
    "They serve the same purpose as `@PostConstruct`/`@PreDestroy` but via XML — useful when you cannot modify source code. **Execution order**: `@PostConstruct` → `InitializingBean.afterPropertiesSet()` → `init-method`.",
    "",
    "### 26. What is the use of FactoryBean in Spring?",
    "",
    "`FactoryBean<T>` customizes complex bean creation logic. The container calls `getObject()` to produce the actual bean. Use `context.getBean(\"name\")` for the produced object and `context.getBean(\"&name\")` for the factory itself. Spring uses this internally for `ProxyFactoryBean`, `LocalSessionFactoryBean`, etc.",
    "",
    "### 27. What is the use of ApplicationContextAware interface?",
    "",
    "It provides a bean with a reference to the `ApplicationContext` for programmatic bean lookup, resource access, or event publishing. **Prefer `@Autowired` injection** in most cases — use `ApplicationContextAware` only for dynamic/runtime bean resolution.",
    "",
    "### 28. What is the use of BeanPostProcessor interface?",
    "",
    "`BeanPostProcessor` modifies or wraps bean **instances** during initialization. It intercepts **every bean** in the container with `postProcessBeforeInitialization()` and `postProcessAfterInitialization()`. Spring uses it internally for `@Autowired` processing, `@PostConstruct` handling, and AOP proxy creation.",
    "",
    "### 29. What is the use of BeanFactoryPostProcessor interface?",
    "",
    "`BeanFactoryPostProcessor` modifies bean **definitions** (metadata) **before** any beans are instantiated. It can alter properties, scope, or lazy-init settings. Spring's `PropertySourcesPlaceholderConfigurer` (resolving `${...}` placeholders) is a well-known example.",
    "",
    "### 30. What is the difference between ApplicationContext and WebApplicationContext?",
    "",
    "| Aspect | ApplicationContext | WebApplicationContext |",
    "|---|---|---|",
    "| **Scope** | General-purpose | Web applications only |",
    "| **Servlet Access** | No `ServletContext` | Has `getServletContext()` |",
    "| **Bean Scopes** | `singleton`, `prototype` | Adds `request`, `session`, `application`, `websocket` |",
    "| **Hierarchy** | Root context | Child of root (inherits beans) |",
    "",
    "In a typical Spring web app, the root `ApplicationContext` holds services/repositories and the child `WebApplicationContext` (created by `DispatcherServlet`) holds controllers/view resolvers."
  ].join("\n"),

  "Spring Boot": [
    "## Spring Boot",
    "",
    "### 1. What is Spring Boot?",
    "",
    "Spring Boot is an opinionated framework built on top of the Spring Framework that simplifies the creation of production-ready applications. It follows **convention over configuration**, providing auto-configuration, embedded servers (Tomcat/Jetty/Undertow), and starter dependencies so developers can get started with minimal setup.",
    "",
    "### 2. What are the advantages of using Spring Boot?",
    "",
    "- **Auto-configuration** — automatically configures beans based on classpath dependencies",
    "- **Embedded servers** — no need to deploy WAR files",
    "- **Starter dependencies** — curated dependency sets with compatible versions",
    "- **Production-ready features** — health checks, metrics via Actuator",
    "- **No XML required** — annotation-driven configuration",
    "- **Rapid development** — Spring Initializr generates runnable projects in seconds",
    "- **Opinionated defaults** that can be overridden when needed",
    "",
    "### 3. What are the key features of Spring Boot?",
    "",
    "Auto-configuration, starter POMs, embedded server, Spring Boot Actuator, Spring Boot CLI, externalized configuration (`application.properties`/`application.yml`/env vars), Spring Boot DevTools (auto-restart, live reload).",
    "",
    "### 4. What is the difference between Spring and Spring Boot?",
    "",
    "| Aspect | Spring Framework | Spring Boot |",
    "|---|---|---|",
    "| Configuration | Requires explicit config | Auto-configuration with defaults |",
    "| Server | External server (WAR deploy) | Embedded server (executable JAR) |",
    "| Dependencies | Manual version management | Starter dependencies |",
    "| Boilerplate | Significant | Minimal |",
    "| Production features | Manual setup | Actuator built-in |",
    "",
    "Spring Boot is a layer on top that removes the ceremony of configuring Spring.",
    "",
    "### 5. What is Spring Boot Starter?",
    "",
    "A Starter is a dependency descriptor that bundles related libraries needed for a capability. Add one starter and get all transitive dependencies with compatible versions: `spring-boot-starter-web` (Tomcat + Spring MVC + Jackson), `spring-boot-starter-data-jpa` (Hibernate + HikariCP), `spring-boot-starter-test` (JUnit 5 + Mockito + AssertJ).",
    "",
    "### 6. What is Spring Boot Auto Configuration?",
    "",
    "Auto-configuration creates and configures beans based on classpath JARs and defined properties. It uses `@Conditional` annotations (`@ConditionalOnClass`, `@ConditionalOnMissingBean`, `@ConditionalOnProperty`). **Your beans always win** — if you define a bean of the same type, auto-configuration backs off. Debug with `--debug` flag or `/actuator/conditions`.",
    "",
    "### 7. What is Spring Boot CLI?",
    "",
    "A command-line tool for quickly prototyping Spring apps using Groovy scripts. Run `spring run hello.groovy` — dependencies are resolved automatically. Useful for prototyping, not for production.",
    "",
    "### 8. What is Spring Initializer?",
    "",
    "Spring Initializr ([start.spring.io](https://start.spring.io)) is a web-based project generator. Select build tool, language, Spring Boot version, and starters — it generates a downloadable ZIP with project structure and a ready-to-run main class. Also available via IDE and CLI.",
    "",
    "### 9. What is Spring Boot Actuator?",
    "",
    "Actuator provides production-ready endpoints for monitoring: `/actuator/health` (application health), `/actuator/metrics` (JVM and custom metrics), `/actuator/info` (application info), `/actuator/env` (environment properties). Supports custom health indicators and integrates with Prometheus/Grafana.",
    "",
    "### 10. What is the use of @SpringBootApplication annotation?",
    "",
    "It combines three annotations: `@Configuration` (bean definition source), `@EnableAutoConfiguration` (enables auto-config), and `@ComponentScan` (scans current package and sub-packages). Place it on your main class in the root package.",
    "",
    "### 11. What is the use of @EnableAutoConfiguration annotation?",
    "",
    "Tells Spring Boot to auto-configure beans based on classpath dependencies. Already included in `@SpringBootApplication`. Exclude specific auto-configs with `exclude = {DataSourceAutoConfiguration.class}`.",
    "",
    "### 12. What is the use of @ComponentScan annotation?",
    "",
    "Tells Spring which packages to scan for `@Component`, `@Service`, `@Repository`, `@Controller` classes. Included in `@SpringBootApplication`, which scans the annotated class's package and sub-packages by default.",
    "",
    "### 13. What is the use of @Configuration annotation in Spring Boot?",
    "",
    "Marks a class as a source of `@Bean` definitions. Spring creates a CGLIB proxy to ensure singleton semantics on inter-bean method calls. Use it for custom beans that auto-configuration doesn't cover (e.g., `RestTemplate`, `ObjectMapper`).",
    "",
    "### 14. How do you create a Spring Boot application?",
    "",
    "Use Spring Initializr ([start.spring.io](https://start.spring.io)), IDE integration, Spring Boot CLI (`spring init`), or manually create a Gradle/Maven project with `spring-boot-starter-parent` and a `@SpringBootApplication` main class.",
    "",
    "### 15. How do you run a Spring Boot application?",
    "",
    "From IDE (run `main()`), Maven (`./mvnw spring-boot:run`), Gradle (`./gradlew bootRun`), or as an executable JAR (`java -jar target/my-app.jar`).",
    "",
    "### 16. How do you change the default port in Spring Boot?",
    "",
    "`server.port=9090` in `application.properties`, `--server.port=9090` via command line, `SERVER_PORT=9090` env var, or programmatically. Set `server.port=0` for a random port.",
    "",
    "### 17. How do you disable a specific auto-configuration in Spring Boot?",
    "",
    "```java",
    "@SpringBootApplication(exclude = {DataSourceAutoConfiguration.class})",
    "```",
    "",
    "Or via properties: `spring.autoconfigure.exclude=org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.jdbc.DataSourceAutoConfiguration`",
    "",
    "### 18. What are the different ways to provide configuration to a Spring Boot application?",
    "",
    "Precedence (highest first): command-line args → Java system properties → OS env vars → profile-specific properties → `application.properties`/`application.yml` → `@PropertySource` → default properties.",
    "",
    "### 19. What is application.properties file in Spring Boot?",
    "",
    "The default configuration file at `src/main/resources`. Uses `key=value` format for server settings, database connection, JPA, logging, and custom properties. Loaded automatically at startup.",
    "",
    "### 20. What is application.yml file in Spring Boot?",
    "",
    "YAML alternative to `application.properties` with hierarchical format. Avoids prefix repetition, supports multi-profile blocks with `---` separators, and native list/map syntax. Both formats are functionally equivalent; `.properties` takes precedence when both exist.",
    "",
    "### 21. How do you profile configuration in Spring Boot?",
    "",
    "Create `application-{profile}.properties` files (e.g., `application-dev.properties`, `application-prod.properties`). Activate with `spring.profiles.active=dev` in properties, `--spring.profiles.active=prod` via command line, or `SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=prod` env var. Use `@Profile(\"dev\")` to conditionally register beans.",
    "",
    "### 22. What is the use of @Value annotation in Spring Boot?",
    "",
    "Injects individual property values: `@Value(\"${app.name}\")`. Supports defaults (`@Value(\"${timeout:5000}\")`), SpEL expressions, and environment variables. Best for a small number of individual values; use `@ConfigurationProperties` for groups.",
    "",
    "### 23. What is the use of @ConfigurationProperties annotation?",
    "",
    "Binds a group of related properties to a type-safe POJO with `@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = \"app.mail\")`. Supports `@Validated` with Jakarta annotations for startup validation, IDE autocomplete, and nested objects. Preferred over `@Value` for structured configuration.",
    "",
    "### 24. How do you connect Spring Boot to a database?",
    "",
    "Add `spring-boot-starter-data-jpa` + JDBC driver dependency, then configure `spring.datasource.url`, `username`, `password` in `application.properties`. Spring Boot auto-configures `DataSource` (HikariCP), `EntityManagerFactory`, and `TransactionManager`.",
    "",
    "### 25. What is Spring Data JPA in Spring Boot?",
    "",
    "Generates repository implementations from interface definitions. Define method signatures like `findByEmail(String email)` and Spring writes the SQL. Supports derived queries, `@Query` for custom JPQL/native SQL, and pagination/sorting out of the box.",
    "",
    "### 26. What is the use of @Entity annotation?",
    "",
    "Maps a Java class to a database table. Each instance = a row, each field = a column. Requires `@Id` for primary key and a no-arg constructor.",
    "",
    "### 27. What is the use of @Repository annotation?",
    "",
    "Specialization of `@Component` for data access. Enables exception translation (vendor-specific exceptions → `DataAccessException`). When using Spring Data JPA interfaces, `@Repository` is implicit.",
    "",
    "### 28. What is the use of CrudRepository and JpaRepository?",
    "",
    "`CrudRepository` provides basic CRUD operations. `JpaRepository` extends it with pagination, sorting, batch operations, flush, and query-by-example. **Use `JpaRepository` by default.**",
    "",
    "| Feature | CrudRepository | JpaRepository |",
    "|---|---|---|",
    "| Basic CRUD | ✅ | ✅ |",
    "| Pagination/Sorting | ❌ | ✅ |",
    "| Batch operations | ❌ | ✅ |",
    "| Return types | `Iterable` | `List` |",
    "",
    "### 29. How do you handle exceptions in Spring Boot?",
    "",
    "Use `@RestControllerAdvice` with `@ExceptionHandler` methods for global exception handling. Define custom exception classes with `@ResponseStatus`. Catch `MethodArgumentNotValidException` for validation errors. Return structured error response objects.",
    "",
    "### 30. What is the use of @ControllerAdvice annotation?",
    "",
    "Declares a global handler for all controllers. Centralizes exception handling, model attributes, and data binding. `@RestControllerAdvice` combines it with `@ResponseBody` for REST APIs. Can be scoped to specific packages with `basePackages`.",
    "",
    "### 31. What is the use of @RestController annotation?",
    "",
    "`@RestController` = `@Controller` + `@ResponseBody`. Every method returns data (serialized to JSON) instead of a view name. Use for REST APIs; use `@Controller` for server-rendered HTML pages.",
    "",
    "### 32. What is the use of @RequestMapping annotation?",
    "",
    "Maps HTTP requests to handler methods by URL, HTTP method, content type, and headers. Applied at class level (base path) and method level (specific path). In practice, shortcut annotations (`@GetMapping`, etc.) are preferred.",
    "",
    "### 33. What is the use of @GetMapping, @PostMapping, @PutMapping, @DeleteMapping?",
    "",
    "Shortcut annotations for `@RequestMapping` restricted to a specific HTTP method. `@GetMapping(\"/path\")` equals `@RequestMapping(value=\"/path\", method=GET)`. Improves readability.",
    "",
    "### 34. What is the use of @PathVariable and @RequestParam?",
    "",
    "`@PathVariable` extracts from URL path (`/users/{id}`). `@RequestParam` extracts from query string (`?key=val`). `@RequestParam` supports `required` and `defaultValue`. Convention: path variables identify resources, query params filter/modify.",
    "",
    "### 35. What is the use of @ResponseBody annotation?",
    "",
    "Serializes the return value directly to the HTTP response body (JSON/XML) via `HttpMessageConverter`, bypassing view resolution. Implicit in `@RestController`.",
    "",
    "### 36. What is the use of @RequestBody annotation?",
    "",
    "Deserializes the HTTP request body (JSON) into a Java object. Used with POST/PUT/PATCH. Requires `Content-Type: application/json`. Combine with `@Valid` for validation.",
    "",
    "### 37. What is the use of @Valid annotation?",
    "",
    "Triggers Jakarta Bean Validation on the parameter. Constraints like `@NotBlank`, `@Email`, `@Min` on DTO fields are checked before the method executes. Failures throw `MethodArgumentNotValidException` (400).",
    "",
    "### 38. How do you implement logging in Spring Boot?",
    "",
    "Spring Boot uses SLF4J + Logback by default. Use `LoggerFactory.getLogger(MyClass.class)` or Lombok's `@Slf4j`. Configure levels in `application.properties`: `logging.level.com.example=DEBUG`. Use `{}` placeholders for efficient message formatting.",
    "",
    "### 39. What is the default logging framework in Spring Boot?",
    "",
    "**SLF4J** as the API facade with **Logback** as the implementation, included transitively via every starter. Spring Boot bridges other logging frameworks (JUL, Log4j, Commons Logging) to SLF4J. Switch to Log4j2 by excluding `spring-boot-starter-logging` and adding `spring-boot-starter-log4j2`.",
    "",
    "### 40. How do you monitor a Spring Boot application?",
    "",
    "Spring Boot Actuator exposes `/actuator/health`, `/actuator/metrics`, `/actuator/prometheus`. Integrate with Micrometer for Prometheus/Grafana, Spring Boot Admin for web UI, or Micrometer Tracing for distributed tracing with Zipkin/Jaeger.",
    "",
    "### 41. What is the use of Spring Boot DevTools?",
    "",
    "Provides **automatic restart** (uses two classloaders — only your code is reloaded), **LiveReload** (auto-refreshes browser), and **development-friendly defaults** (disables template caching). Automatically disabled in production builds.",
    "",
    "### 42. How do you secure a Spring Boot application?",
    "",
    "Add `spring-boot-starter-security` — all endpoints are secured with HTTP Basic by default. Customize with a `SecurityFilterChain` bean: `.requestMatchers(\"/public/**\").permitAll()`, `.requestMatchers(\"/admin/**\").hasRole(\"ADMIN\")`, `.sessionManagement(STATELESS)` for JWT.",
    "",
    "### 43. What is Spring Security in Spring Boot?",
    "",
    "Framework providing authentication, authorization, and protection against common attacks. Auto-configures all endpoints as secured, default user \"user\" with generated password, CSRF protection, and security headers. Customize with `SecurityFilterChain`, `UserDetailsService`, and `PasswordEncoder` beans.",
    "",
    "### 44. How do you test a Spring Boot application?",
    "",
    "`spring-boot-starter-test` includes JUnit 5, Mockito, AssertJ, Spring Test, MockMvc. Use **`@SpringBootTest`** for full integration tests, **`@WebMvcTest`** for controller-only tests with MockMvc, **`@DataJpaTest`** for repository tests with embedded DB, and **`@MockBean`** to replace beans with mocks.",
    "",
    "### 45. What is the use of @SpringBootTest annotation?",
    "",
    "Creates the **full application context** for integration testing. Options: `MOCK` (mock servlet, use MockMvc), `RANDOM_PORT` (real server, use TestRestTemplate), `DEFINED_PORT`, `NONE`. Use sparingly — prefer slice annotations for faster, focused tests."
  ].join("\n"),

  "Spring AOP": [
    "## Spring AOP",
    "",
    "### 1. What is AOP in Spring?",
    "",
    "**Aspect-Oriented Programming** separates cross-cutting concerns from business logic. Instead of scattering logging, security, or transaction code across every service method, you define that behavior once in an **aspect** and apply it declaratively via pointcut expressions. Spring AOP uses runtime proxies to intercept method calls.",
    "",
    "```java",
    "@Aspect",
    "@Component",
    "public class LoggingAspect {",
    "    @Before(\"execution(* com.example.service.*.*(..))\")",
    "    public void logBefore(JoinPoint jp) {",
    "        System.out.println(\"Calling: \" + jp.getSignature());",
    "    }",
    "}",
    "```",
    "",
    "### 2. What are cross-cutting concerns?",
    "",
    "Behaviors that span multiple layers and cannot be cleanly modularized with standard OOP: **logging**, **security**, **transaction management**, **caching**, **exception handling**, **performance monitoring**. Without AOP, these concerns lead to duplicated, tangled code.",
    "",
    "### 3. What are the different types of advice in Spring AOP?",
    "",
    "| Advice | Annotation | When It Runs |",
    "|---|---|---|",
    "| **Before** | `@Before` | Before the target method |",
    "| **After Returning** | `@AfterReturning` | After successful return |",
    "| **After Throwing** | `@AfterThrowing` | After an exception |",
    "| **After (Finally)** | `@After` | After method completes (success or failure) |",
    "| **Around** | `@Around` | Wraps the method; controls execution |",
    "",
    "### 4. What is the difference between @Before and @After annotations?",
    "",
    "`@Before` runs **before** the target method — used for validation, security checks, logging entry. `@After` runs **after** the method regardless of outcome (like a `finally` block) — used for cleanup and logging exit. `@After` cannot access the return value (use `@AfterReturning` for that).",
    "",
    "### 5. What is the use of @Around advice?",
    "",
    "`@Around` is the most powerful advice. It wraps the entire method using `ProceedingJoinPoint`, giving control to: execute code before/after, skip execution, retry on failure, modify arguments, modify the return value, and measure execution time.",
    "",
    "```java",
    "@Around(\"execution(* com.example.service.*.*(..))\")",
    "public Object measure(ProceedingJoinPoint pjp) throws Throwable {",
    "    long start = System.currentTimeMillis();",
    "    Object result = pjp.proceed();",
    "    System.out.println(pjp.getSignature() + \" took \" + (System.currentTimeMillis() - start) + \"ms\");",
    "    return result;",
    "}",
    "```",
    "",
    "### 6. What is a JoinPoint in Spring AOP?",
    "",
    "A JoinPoint represents a point during execution where an aspect can be applied — always **method executions** in Spring AOP. The `JoinPoint` interface provides metadata: `getSignature()` (method name), `getArgs()` (arguments), `getTarget()` (target object). `ProceedingJoinPoint` extends it with `proceed()` for `@Around` only.",
    "",
    "### 7. What is a Pointcut in Spring AOP?",
    "",
    "A Pointcut is an expression that selects which joinpoints an advice applies to. Common designators: `execution()` (method pattern), `within()` (class/package), `@annotation()` (annotated methods), `bean()` (by bean name). Combine with `&&`, `||`, `!`.",
    "",
    "```java",
    "@Pointcut(\"execution(* com.example.service.*.*(..))\")",
    "public void serviceMethods() {}",
    "",
    "@Before(\"serviceMethods() && !execution(* get*(..))\")",
    "public void logNonGetters(JoinPoint jp) { ... }",
    "```",
    "",
    "### 8. What is an Aspect in Spring AOP?",
    "",
    "An Aspect is a class that encapsulates a cross-cutting concern by combining **pointcuts** (where) with **advice** (what). Annotated with `@Aspect` + `@Component`. Each concern gets its own aspect: `LoggingAspect`, `SecurityAspect`, `CachingAspect`.",
    "",
    "### 9. What is the use of @Aspect annotation?",
    "",
    "Marks a class as an AOP aspect so Spring recognizes its advice and pointcut definitions. Requires `@Component` for bean registration and `@EnableAspectJAutoProxy` on a config class (auto-enabled in Spring Boot with `spring-boot-starter-aop`).",
    "",
    "### 10. What is the difference between Spring AOP and AspectJ?",
    "",
    "| Feature | Spring AOP | AspectJ |",
    "|---|---|---|",
    "| **Weaving** | Runtime (proxy-based) | Compile/load-time (bytecode) |",
    "| **Joinpoints** | Method execution only | Methods, constructors, fields |",
    "| **Self-invocation** | Not intercepted | Intercepted |",
    "| **Final classes** | Cannot proxy (CGLIB) | Can weave |",
    "| **Performance** | Slight proxy overhead | Zero runtime overhead |",
    "| **Complexity** | Simple, no tooling | Requires ajc compiler |",
    "",
    "Spring AOP covers 90%+ of use cases. Use AspectJ only for non-public methods, self-invocations, or non-Spring objects.",
    "",
    "### 11. What is weaving in AOP?",
    "",
    "Weaving is the process of linking aspects with target objects. Spring AOP uses **runtime weaving** — when a bean is created, Spring wraps it in a proxy that intercepts method calls and invokes advice. Other types: **compile-time** (AspectJ compiler) and **load-time** (Java agent).",
    "",
    "### 12. What is proxy in Spring AOP?",
    "",
    "A proxy wraps the target bean to intercept method calls. **JDK Dynamic Proxy**: used when the bean implements an interface. **CGLIB Proxy**: creates a subclass (Spring Boot default). Cannot proxy `final` classes/methods. External calls go through the proxy; internal (`this.method()`) calls bypass it.",
    "",
    "### 13. What are the limitations of Spring AOP?",
    "",
    "1. **Method execution only** — no field/constructor interception",
    "2. **Public methods only** (effectively)",
    "3. **Self-invocation not intercepted** — `this.method()` bypasses proxy",
    "4. **Spring beans only** — can't advise non-managed objects",
    "5. **No final classes/methods** with CGLIB",
    "6. **Slight runtime overhead** per proxy call",
    "7. **Limited pointcut expressiveness** compared to full AspectJ"
  ].join("\n"),

  "Spring JDBC": [
    "## Spring JDBC",
    "",
    "### 1. What is Spring JDBC?",
    "",
    "Spring JDBC simplifies database access by eliminating JDBC boilerplate — connection management, statement creation, result set iteration, exception handling, and resource cleanup. The `spring-jdbc` module provides `JdbcTemplate`, `NamedParameterJdbcTemplate`, `SimpleJdbcInsert`/`SimpleJdbcCall`, and automatic exception translation.",
    "",
    "### 2. What is JdbcTemplate in Spring?",
    "",
    "The core class in Spring JDBC. **Thread-safe**, instantiated once and shared. Handles connection acquisition, statement creation, execution, result set processing, exception translation, and resource cleanup.",
    "",
    "```java",
    "@Repository",
    "public class UserRepository {",
    "    private final JdbcTemplate jdbc;",
    "",
    "    public UserRepository(JdbcTemplate jdbc) { this.jdbc = jdbc; }",
    "",
    "    public User findById(Long id) {",
    "        return jdbc.queryForObject(\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?\",",
    "            (rs, rowNum) -> new User(rs.getLong(\"id\"), rs.getString(\"name\")), id);",
    "    }",
    "",
    "    public int save(User u) {",
    "        return jdbc.update(\"INSERT INTO users (name, email) VALUES (?, ?)\", u.getName(), u.getEmail());",
    "    }",
    "}",
    "```",
    "",
    "### 3. What are the advantages of using JdbcTemplate?",
    "",
    "- Automatic connection, statement, and resource management",
    "- Automatic exception translation (`SQLException` → `DataAccessException`)",
    "- Thread-safe — one instance for the whole application",
    "- Integrates with Spring's `@Transactional`",
    "- Reduces code by 60-80% compared to raw JDBC",
    "- Parameter binding prevents SQL injection",
    "",
    "### 4. What is the difference between JdbcTemplate and NamedParameterJdbcTemplate?",
    "",
    "`JdbcTemplate` uses positional `?` parameters. `NamedParameterJdbcTemplate` uses `:name` parameters — more readable with many params and supports `IN` clauses with `List` values directly.",
    "",
    "```java",
    "// JdbcTemplate: \"WHERE dept = ? AND salary > ?\"",
    "// NamedParameterJdbcTemplate: \"WHERE dept = :dept AND salary > :minSalary\"",
    "// IN clause: \"WHERE id IN (:ids)\" with .addValue(\"ids\", List.of(1, 2, 3))",
    "```",
    "",
    "### 5. What is RowMapper in Spring JDBC?",
    "",
    "Maps **one row** of a `ResultSet` to **one object**. Called once per row. Define as a class, lambda, or use `BeanPropertyRowMapper` for automatic column-to-property matching (supports `snake_case` → `camelCase`).",
    "",
    "```java",
    "List<User> users = jdbc.query(\"SELECT * FROM users\",",
    "    (rs, rowNum) -> new User(rs.getLong(\"id\"), rs.getString(\"name\")));",
    "```",
    "",
    "### 6. What is ResultSetExtractor in Spring JDBC?",
    "",
    "Processes the **entire ResultSet** in one call — you control the `while(rs.next())` loop. Ideal for complex mappings like one-to-many relationships where `RowMapper` would produce duplicates.",
    "",
    "| | RowMapper | ResultSetExtractor |",
    "|---|---|---|",
    "| Called | Once per row | Once for entire ResultSet |",
    "| Best for | Flat list of objects | Aggregated/grouped results, one-to-many |",
    "",
    "### 7. How do you handle exceptions in Spring JDBC?",
    "",
    "Spring automatically translates `SQLException` into a rich hierarchy of unchecked `DataAccessException` subtypes: `DuplicateKeyException`, `DataIntegrityViolationException`, `EmptyResultDataAccessException`, `BadSqlGrammarException`, etc. This makes exception handling vendor-agnostic.",
    "",
    "### 8. How do you perform batch updates in Spring JDBC?",
    "",
    "`batchUpdate()` sends multiple statements in one round-trip. Use `BatchPreparedStatementSetter` with `JdbcTemplate` or `SqlParameterSourceUtils.createBatch()` with `NamedParameterJdbcTemplate`. For very large batches, process in chunks.",
    "",
    "### 9. How do you connect to a database using Spring JDBC?",
    "",
    "In **Spring Boot**: configure `spring.datasource.url`, `username`, `password` in `application.properties` — Spring auto-configures `DataSource` and `JdbcTemplate`. In **plain Spring**: define `DataSource` and `JdbcTemplate` beans manually via `@Configuration`.",
    "",
    "### 10. What is the use of DataSource in Spring JDBC?",
    "",
    "`DataSource` (`javax.sql.DataSource`) is a factory for database connections. It enables connection pooling (critical for performance), configuration externalization, transaction integration, and testability. **HikariCP** is the Spring Boot default pool. Spring's `@Transactional` acquires/releases connections from the pool automatically."
  ].join("\n"),

  "Spring Hibernate": [
    "## Spring Hibernate",
    "",
    "### 1. What is Hibernate?",
    "",
    "Hibernate is an open-source **ORM (Object-Relational Mapping)** framework that maps Java objects to database tables. It implements the JPA specification and handles all low-level JDBC interactions — you work with objects and Hibernate generates SQL.",
    "",
    "### 2. What are the advantages of using Hibernate?",
    "",
    "Eliminates SQL boilerplate, provides database portability via dialects, automatic schema generation (`hbm2ddl.auto`), L1/L2 caching, lazy loading, HQL/JPQL for database-independent queries, dirty checking (auto-detects changed entities), and seamless integration with Spring's `@Transactional`.",
    "",
    "### 3. What is ORM in Hibernate?",
    "",
    "ORM maps Java concepts to relational concepts: Class → Table, Object → Row, Field → Column, Relationship (`@OneToMany`) → Foreign Key. Hibernate reads annotations to define these correspondences and generates SQL automatically.",
    "",
    "### 4. What is the difference between Hibernate and JDBC?",
    "",
    "| Aspect | JDBC | Hibernate |",
    "|---|---|---|",
    "| SQL | Write manually | Generated automatically |",
    "| Result mapping | Manual `ResultSet` → Object | Automatic via annotations |",
    "| Caching | None | L1 + L2 |",
    "| Lazy loading | Not supported | Built-in |",
    "| Schema generation | Manual DDL | Auto via `hbm2ddl.auto` |",
    "",
    "### 5. What is HibernateTemplate in Spring?",
    "",
    "A **legacy** helper class (pre-Spring 3.x) that simplified Session management. **Modern code should use `SessionFactory.getCurrentSession()` directly** within `@Transactional` methods.",
    "",
    "### 6. How do you integrate Hibernate with Spring?",
    "",
    "Configure three beans: `DataSource`, `LocalSessionFactoryBean` (with `packagesToScan` and Hibernate properties), and `HibernateTransactionManager`. Enable `@EnableTransactionManagement`. With Spring Boot + JPA, this is auto-configured.",
    "",
    "### 7. What is the use of SessionFactory in Hibernate?",
    "",
    "`SessionFactory` is a **heavyweight, thread-safe, one-per-app** factory holding compiled mappings and the L2 cache. `Session` is a **lightweight, one-per-transaction** unit of work containing the L1 cache. Use `sessionFactory.getCurrentSession()` within `@Transactional` methods.",
    "",
    "### 8. What is the use of @Entity annotation in Hibernate?",
    "",
    "Marks a class as a JPA entity mapped to a database table. Requires a no-arg constructor and an `@Id` field.",
    "",
    "### 9. What is the use of @Table annotation in Hibernate?",
    "",
    "Customizes the table mapping: `@Table(name = \"customers\", schema = \"sales\", uniqueConstraints = ...)`. Without it, the table name defaults to the class name.",
    "",
    "### 10. What is the use of @Id annotation in Hibernate?",
    "",
    "Marks the primary key field. Every entity must have exactly one `@Id` (or `@EmbeddedId`/`@IdClass` for composite keys).",
    "",
    "### 11. What is the use of @GeneratedValue annotation?",
    "",
    "Auto-generates primary key values. Strategies: **IDENTITY** (auto-increment, MySQL/PostgreSQL), **SEQUENCE** (database sequence, preferred for high-throughput), **TABLE** (portable but slow), **AUTO** (provider chooses).",
    "",
    "### 12. What is the difference between get() and load() methods in Hibernate?",
    "",
    "| | `get()` | `load()` |",
    "|---|---|---|",
    "| Return | Actual object or `null` | Proxy (never null) |",
    "| DB hit | Immediate | Deferred until property access |",
    "| Not found | Returns `null` | Throws `ObjectNotFoundException` on access |",
    "",
    "`load()` is useful for setting foreign key references without loading the full entity.",
    "",
    "### 13. What is the difference between save(), persist(), and saveOrUpdate() methods?",
    "",
    "| Method | Return | Behavior | Standard |",
    "|---|---|---|---|",
    "| `save()` | Generated ID | Always inserts | Hibernate-specific |",
    "| `persist()` | `void` | Inserts within transaction | JPA standard |",
    "| `saveOrUpdate()` | `void` | Inserts or updates based on ID | Hibernate-specific |",
    "",
    "**Best practice**: Use `persist()` for new entities, `merge()` for detached entities.",
    "",
    "### 14. What is lazy loading in Hibernate?",
    "",
    "Defers fetching of associated entities until accessed. Default for collections (`@OneToMany`, `@ManyToMany`): `LAZY`. Default for single associations (`@ManyToOne`, `@OneToOne`): `EAGER`.",
    "",
    "**N+1 problem**: Loading N parents triggers N additional queries for children. Fix with `JOIN FETCH` in JPQL, `@EntityGraph`, or `@BatchSize`.",
    "",
    "**`LazyInitializationException`**: Occurs when accessing a lazy association after the Session closes. Ensure access within `@Transactional` boundaries.",
    "",
    "### 15. What is the use of Hibernate Validator?",
    "",
    "Reference implementation of Jakarta Bean Validation. Provides constraint annotations (`@NotNull`, `@NotBlank`, `@Email`, `@Size`, `@Min`, `@Pattern`, `@Past`, `@Future`) for declarative validation. In Spring MVC, triggers automatically with `@Valid` on controller parameters."
  ].join("\n"),

  "Spring MVC": [
    "## Spring MVC",
    "",
    "### 1. What is Spring MVC?",
    "",
    "A web framework built on the Servlet API implementing the **Model-View-Controller** pattern. It provides clean separation between presentation, business logic, and data, using `DispatcherServlet` as a front controller to handle routing, parameter binding, validation, and view resolution.",
    "",
    "### 2. What are the components of Spring MVC architecture?",
    "",
    "1. **DispatcherServlet** — front controller, dispatches requests",
    "2. **HandlerMapping** — maps URLs to controller methods",
    "3. **Controller** — processes request, returns model + view name",
    "4. **ViewResolver** — resolves view name to actual view",
    "5. **View** — renders the response (HTML, JSON)",
    "",
    "Flow: `Client → DispatcherServlet → HandlerMapping → Controller → ViewResolver → View → Response`",
    "",
    "### 3. What is DispatcherServlet in Spring MVC?",
    "",
    "The **front controller** — single entry point for all HTTP requests. Coordinates handler lookup, execution, view resolution, and response rendering. Auto-configured in Spring Boot, mapped to `/` by default.",
    "",
    "### 4. What is the role of HandlerMapping in Spring MVC?",
    "",
    "Determines which controller method handles a request URL. `RequestMappingHandlerMapping` (default) maps based on `@RequestMapping` annotations. Spring builds a routing table at startup.",
    "",
    "### 5. What is the role of Controller in Spring MVC?",
    "",
    "Processes requests, invokes business logic, populates the model, and returns either a view name (server-rendered pages) or response body (REST APIs).",
    "",
    "### 6. What is the role of ViewResolver in Spring MVC?",
    "",
    "Maps logical view names (e.g., `\"home\"`) to actual view files (e.g., `/WEB-INF/views/home.jsp`). Common implementations: `InternalResourceViewResolver` (JSP), `ThymeleafViewResolver`.",
    "",
    "### 7. What is the role of View in Spring MVC?",
    "",
    "Renders model data into the response. Different implementations: `JstlView` (JSP), `ThymeleafView` (Thymeleaf), `MappingJackson2JsonView` (JSON).",
    "",
    "### 8. What is the use of @Controller annotation?",
    "",
    "Specialization of `@Component` for Spring MVC controllers. Handler methods return **view names** resolved by `ViewResolver`.",
    "",
    "### 9. What is the use of @RestController annotation?",
    "",
    "`@Controller` + `@ResponseBody`. Every method returns data serialized to JSON, not view names. Used for REST APIs.",
    "",
    "### 10. What is the use of @RequestMapping annotation?",
    "",
    "Maps HTTP requests to handlers by URL, HTTP method, headers, and content type. Applied at class level (base path) and method level.",
    "",
    "### 11. What is the difference between @RequestMapping and @GetMapping?",
    "",
    "`@GetMapping` is a shortcut for `@RequestMapping(method = RequestMethod.GET)`. Same for `@PostMapping`, `@PutMapping`, `@DeleteMapping`, `@PatchMapping`. The shortcuts are preferred for readability.",
    "",
    "### 12. What is the use of @ModelAttribute annotation?",
    "",
    "**On a parameter**: binds request parameters (form fields) to an object. **On a method**: adds attributes to the Model for all handler methods in the controller (e.g., dropdown values).",
    "",
    "### 13. What is the use of @RequestParam annotation?",
    "",
    "Extracts query string parameters: `@RequestParam String name` from `?name=John`. Supports `required`, `defaultValue`.",
    "",
    "### 14. What is the use of @PathVariable annotation?",
    "",
    "Extracts values from URL path segments: `@PathVariable Long id` from `/users/{id}`. Values are mandatory since they're part of the URI structure.",
    "",
    "### 15. What is the use of @ResponseBody annotation?",
    "",
    "Serializes the return value to the HTTP response body (JSON/XML), bypassing view resolution. Implicit in `@RestController`.",
    "",
    "### 16. What is the use of @RequestBody annotation?",
    "",
    "Deserializes the HTTP request body (JSON) into a Java object. Used with POST/PUT. Combine with `@Valid` for validation.",
    "",
    "### 17. What is the use of @Valid annotation in Spring MVC?",
    "",
    "Triggers Bean Validation on the annotated parameter. Errors are captured in `BindingResult`. For REST APIs, omitting `BindingResult` causes `MethodArgumentNotValidException`.",
    "",
    "### 18. How do you handle form submission in Spring MVC?",
    "",
    "GET handler displays the form with an empty model attribute. POST handler processes with `@Valid @ModelAttribute`. If `BindingResult` has errors, return the form view; otherwise, redirect after save (PRG pattern).",
    "",
    "### 19. How do you perform validation in Spring MVC?",
    "",
    "Three approaches: (1) Bean Validation annotations + `@Valid`, (2) Spring's `Validator` interface for cross-field logic, (3) Custom constraint annotations. All work with `BindingResult`.",
    "",
    "### 20. What is BindingResult in Spring MVC?",
    "",
    "Holds validation and data binding results — field errors, global errors, rejected values. **Must immediately follow the `@Valid`/`@ModelAttribute` parameter** in the method signature. If omitted, Spring throws an exception.",
    "",
    "### 21. How do you create custom validators in Spring MVC?",
    "",
    "**Approach 1**: Implement `Validator` interface with `supports()` and `validate()` for cross-field validation (password match, date ranges, uniqueness checks).",
    "",
    "**Approach 2**: Create a custom constraint annotation with `@Constraint(validatedBy = ...)` backed by a `ConstraintValidator` — reusable across entities.",
    "",
    "### 22. What is the use of Interceptors in Spring MVC?",
    "",
    "Execute logic **before**, **after**, and **upon completion** of controller execution. Used for logging, authentication, locale resolution, performance monitoring. Register via `WebMvcConfigurer.addInterceptors()`.",
    "",
    "### 23. What is HandlerInterceptor in Spring MVC?",
    "",
    "Interface with three methods: `preHandle()` (before controller, return `false` to abort), `postHandle()` (after controller, before view rendering), `afterCompletion()` (after everything, for cleanup/timing).",
    "",
    "### 24. What is the difference between filters and interceptors?",
    "",
    "| Aspect | Filter | HandlerInterceptor |",
    "|---|---|---|",
    "| Spec | Servlet API | Spring MVC |",
    "| Scope | All requests (including static) | Only Spring-dispatched requests |",
    "| Access to | `ServletRequest/Response` only | Handler method, `ModelAndView` |",
    "| Execution | Before DispatcherServlet | After DispatcherServlet, before Controller |",
    "",
    "### 25. How do you configure view resolvers in Spring MVC?",
    "",
    "Define `ViewResolver` beans in `@Configuration` class. Chain multiple resolvers with `setOrder()` (lower = higher priority). Common: `InternalResourceViewResolver` (JSP), `ThymeleafViewResolver`.",
    "",
    "### 26. What is InternalResourceViewResolver?",
    "",
    "Resolves view names to JSP by prepending a **prefix** and appending a **suffix**: `\"home\"` → `/WEB-INF/views/home.jsp`. Placing JSPs under `/WEB-INF/` prevents direct browser access.",
    "",
    "### 27. How do you handle exceptions in Spring MVC?",
    "",
    "Three mechanisms: (1) `@ExceptionHandler` in a controller (local), (2) `@ControllerAdvice` + `@ExceptionHandler` (global), (3) `HandlerExceptionResolver` (low-level). Use `@RestControllerAdvice` for REST APIs.",
    "",
    "### 28. What is @ExceptionHandler annotation?",
    "",
    "Marks a method that handles specific exception types. Can return a view name, `ResponseEntity`, or response body. In a controller, handles that controller's exceptions only; in `@ControllerAdvice`, handles all.",
    "",
    "### 29. What is @ControllerAdvice annotation?",
    "",
    "Declares global exception handlers, model attributes, and data binding initialization for all controllers. `@RestControllerAdvice` = `@ControllerAdvice` + `@ResponseBody`.",
    "",
    "### 30. What is the difference between @Controller and @RestController?",
    "",
    "| Aspect | `@Controller` | `@RestController` |",
    "|---|---|---|",
    "| Returns | View name (HTML) | Response body (JSON) |",
    "| `@ResponseBody` | Per method | Implicit |",
    "| Use case | Server-rendered pages | REST APIs |",
    "",
    "### 31. How do you manage session attributes in Spring MVC?",
    "",
    "Use `HttpSession` directly or `@SessionAttributes` (preferred for multi-step forms). `@SessionAttributes(\"order\")` keeps the attribute in the session across requests. Call `SessionStatus.setComplete()` to clean up.",
    "",
    "### 32. What is the use of @SessionAttributes annotation?",
    "",
    "Declares which model attributes persist in the HTTP session between requests. Essential for multi-step workflows (wizards). Only applies to the controller it's declared on.",
    "",
    "### 33. What is the use of @RequestScope and @SessionScope annotations?",
    "",
    "`@RequestScope` — new bean per HTTP request. `@SessionScope` — new bean per HTTP session. When injected into singletons, Spring uses scoped proxies to ensure the correct scoped instance is used.",
    "",
    "### 34. How do you internationalize a Spring MVC application?",
    "",
    "Three components: **MessageSource** (loads `messages_XX.properties`), **LocaleResolver** (determines locale), **LocaleChangeInterceptor** (switches locale via `?lang=fr`). In views, use `<spring:message code=\"key\"/>` or Thymeleaf's `#{key}`.",
    "",
    "### 35. What is LocaleResolver in Spring MVC?",
    "",
    "Determines the user's locale. Implementations: `SessionLocaleResolver` (most common), `CookieLocaleResolver` (persists across sessions), `AcceptHeaderLocaleResolver` (browser setting, read-only), `FixedLocaleResolver`.",
    "",
    "### 36. What is LocaleChangeInterceptor in Spring MVC?",
    "",
    "Intercepts requests and switches locale based on a request parameter (e.g., `?lang=fr`). Updates the configured `LocaleResolver`. Register via `WebMvcConfigurer.addInterceptors()`."
  ].join("\n")
};

